### 7.7 Statistical uncertainty (bootstrap intervals)

The scores in §2 are point estimates on finite partitions; the
bootstrap confidence intervals beside them (§2.2 test CI, §2.2 dev→test
gap CI, §3.2 dev CI) are percentile bootstraps over the per-row scores
(DESIGN.md §7.1.4). At the small partition sizes `spp` typically runs
(tens of rows), these intervals are **wide** — a single row can move the
metric several points — and that width is the honest signal, replacing
any fixed noise-floor assumption. Read the point estimates with their
intervals: a wide test CI means the result is uncertain at this
test-set size and the remedy is **more labeled data**, not more
iterations. The intervals are descriptive context for a human; they do
not change any score, gate, or verdict.

